{"schema":"cr/v1","tip":"b10eed40630490283cedc0b9eed1c6a02b3a9ca7","base":"fe3697546753ca7a5c8d4f76b9ecab6d3265e5f4","branch":"epic/E-WSANDBOX","committer":"vuluu <quangvu.luu92@gmail.com>","timestamp":1795876826,"verdict":"FAIL","blockers":0,"importants":4,"code_diff_hash":"e8b32979d30f5545f8eb64798446999c3da0fd67","round":1,"scope":"full","findings":[{"location":"scripts/router-worker.sh:829","claim":"Every worker's sandbox gets the one shared $TMPDIR/keel-worker-pnpm as an allowWrite root, and all worktrees' modules-<cksum> package trees and the shared pnpm store live under it, so a worker in worktree A can overwrite worktree B's package tree or the store, and A's code then runs in B's pnpm test/vite build - a cross-worktree write path the W3 fix exists to close.","severity":"important","evidence":"scripts/router-worker.sh:819,826-829,837,876 with tests/suite/router.rs:4436,4609: the one shared $TMPDIR/keel-worker-pnpm (every modules-<cksum> plus the store) is an allowWrite root for every app worktree and app/node_modules links at its own modules dir, so a worker in worktree A can write modules-<cksum B>/.bin or the store and B's granted pnpm test/vite build executes it; the app-symlink guard at scripts/router-worker.sh:806 and tests/suite/router.rs:4659-4660 shows the project already treats cross-worktree package-tree poisoning as real (W3's purpose is the deny/store location, not this, but the exposure stands)."},{"location":"scripts/router-worker.sh:837","claim":"The settings env sets an absolute pnpm_config_modules_dir, so a granted pnpm add/pnpm install run anywhere but the app (the scripts/router-scratch.sh scratch repo, a nested project) installs the package into the app's modules dir and leaves the target directory without node_modules while printing success - the W10 probe had to env -u pnpm_config_modules_dir by hand for exactly this, and no skill or check tells a worker to.","severity":"important","evidence":"scripts/router-worker.sh:837 sets the absol